{"success":true,"data":{"id":"8435df0d-3ef4-4022-8080-d0bd2cbe43e2","pathId":"10dd8542-bb3d-499c-972c-69310b9a2393","pathTitle":"Complete C# Developer Roadmap","moduleId":"6f14ad6c-c9be-48e4-a8f2-d4da33536d6f","moduleTitle":"Language Foundations","title":"Value types and reference types","content":"## Value vs reference semantics\n- **Value types** (`struct`, `enum`, numeric types, `DateTime`) store data directly and are **copied** on assignment.\n- **Reference types** (`class`, `string`, arrays, delegates) store a reference to a heap object; assignment copies the reference.\n\n## Strings are special\n`string` is a reference type with **value equality** and **immutability**: every modification creates a new string.\n\n## Interview tips\n- Explain *boxing*: converting a value type to `object` allocates on the heap.\n- Know when to choose a `struct`: small (≤ 16 bytes), immutable, value-like (e.g. `Point`, `Money`).","type":"Reading","estimatedMinutes":12,"exerciseInstructions":null,"quizId":null,"questions":[{"id":"df46210c-d654-40af-bf10-c2d373068e35","title":"Value types in C#","technology":{"id":"caf787e1-6463-44de-b5d9-993f01b4377d","name":"C#","slug":"csharp"},"category":{"id":"52623613-5a30-455c-a10f-dd61ac05a95e","name":"C# Fundamentals","slug":"csharp-fundamentals"},"difficulty":"Beginner","type":"MultipleChoice","estimatedSeconds":45,"tags":["types","struct"],"isPremium":false,"isBookmarked":false,"lastAttemptCorrect":null},{"id":"49f1f7a7-66e6-4eaf-8b48-794ca2b68f95","title":"String immutability","technology":{"id":"caf787e1-6463-44de-b5d9-993f01b4377d","name":"C#","slug":"csharp"},"category":{"id":"52623613-5a30-455c-a10f-dd61ac05a95e","name":"C# Fundamentals","slug":"csharp-fundamentals"},"difficulty":"Beginner","type":"MultipleChoice","estimatedSeconds":45,"tags":["string","immutability"],"isPremium":false,"isBookmarked":false,"lastAttemptCorrect":null},{"id":"709ee96c-3ca0-4316-a17a-18cb0f6f99a2","title":"Comparing strings with ==","technology":{"id":"c